+bool PrivateBroadcast::Add(const CTransactionRef& tx) EXCLUSIVE_LOCKS_REQUIRED(!m_mutex)
+{
+    const Txid& txid = tx->GetHash();
+    LOCK(m_mutex);
+    auto [pos, inserted] = m_by_txid.emplace(txid, TxWithPriority{.tx = tx, .priority = Priority{}});
+    if (inserted) {
+        m_by_priority.emplace(Priority{}, txid);
+    }
+    return inserted;
+}
+
+std::optional<size_t> PrivateBroadcast::Remove(const CTransactionRef& tx) EXCLUSIVE_LOCKS_REQUIRED(!m_mutex)
+{
+    LOCK(m_mutex);
+    auto iters = Find(tx->GetHash());
+    if (!iters || iters->by_txid->second.tx->GetWitnessHash() != tx->GetWitnessHash()) {
+        return std::nullopt;
+    }
+    const size_t num_broadcasted{iters->by_priority->first.num_broadcasted};
+    m_by_priority.erase(iters->by_priority);
+    m_by_txid.erase(iters->by_txid);
+    return num_broadcasted;
+}
+
+std::optional<CTransactionRef> PrivateBroadcast::GetTxForBroadcast() EXCLUSIVE_LOCKS_REQUIRED(!m_mutex)
+{
+    LOCK(m_mutex);
+    if (m_by_priority.empty()) {
+        return std::nullopt;
+    }
+    const Txid& txid = m_by_priority.begin()->second;
+    auto it = m_by_txid.find(txid);
+    if (Assume(it != m_by_txid.end())) {
+        return it->second.tx;
+    }
+    m_by_priority.erase(m_by_priority.begin());
+    return std::nullopt;
+}
+
+void PrivateBroadcast::PushedToNode(const NodeId& nodeid, const Txid& txid) EXCLUSIVE_LOCKS_REQUIRED(!m_mutex)
+{
+    LOCK(m_mutex);
+    m_by_nodeid.emplace(nodeid, txid);
+}
+
+std::optional<CTransactionRef> PrivateBroadcast::GetTxPushedToNode(const NodeId& nodeid) const
+    EXCLUSIVE_LOCKS_REQUIRED(!m_mutex)
+{
+    LOCK(m_mutex);
+
+    auto it_by_node = m_by_nodeid.find(nodeid);
+    if (it_by_node == m_by_nodeid.end()) {
+        return std::nullopt;
+    }
+    const Txid txid{it_by_node->second};
+
+    auto it_by_txid = m_by_txid.find(txid);
+    if (it_by_txid == m_by_txid.end()) {
+        return std::nullopt;
+    }
+    return it_by_txid->second.tx;
+}
+
+bool PrivateBroadcast::FinishBroadcast(const NodeId& nodeid, bool confirmed_by_node) EXCLUSIVE_LOCKS_REQUIRED(!m_mutex)
+{
+    LOCK(m_mutex);
+    auto it = m_by_nodeid.find(nodeid);
+    if (it == m_by_nodeid.end()) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    const Txid txid{it->second};
+    m_by_nodeid.erase(it);
+
+    auto iters = Find(txid);
+
+    if (!iters.has_value()) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    if (!confirmed_by_node) {
+        return true;
+    }
+
+    // Update broadcast stats, since txid was found and its reception is confirmed by the node.
+
+    Priority& priority = iters->by_txid->second.priority;
+
+    ++priority.num_broadcasted;
+    priority.last_broadcasted = NodeClock::now();
+
+    // Remove and re-add the entry in the m_by_priority map because we have changed the key.
+    m_by_priority.erase(iters->by_priority);
+    m_by_priority.emplace(priority, txid);
+
+    return true;

comments.body:
I like the new version, but I want to give the [extract](https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/container/map/extract) another try, see if you like it more.

With `erase`+`emplace` you destroy the node and construct a new one, with `extract` you detach the existing tree node, mutate the key in the detached node, reinsert the same node. It's not a huge difference, but if there's a dedicated tool for it since C++17, why not use it, it expressed intent more directly.

instead of:
```C++
// Remove and re-add the entry in the m_by_priority map because we have changed the key.
m_by_priority.erase(iters->by_priority);
m_by_priority.emplace(priority, txid);
```
what do you think of:
```C++
auto node_handle{m_by_priority.extract(iters->by_priority)};
node_handle.key() = priority;
m_by_priority.insert(std::move(node_handle));
```

Not sure how that would behave for priority duplicates - is that corner-case covered by tests?

----

Though this begs the question why we're using a red-black tree as a priority queue when there's a dedicated container for that exact scenario.
The answer seems to be that https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/container/priority_queue.html isn't really equipped with `decrease_key` / `increase_key` like e.g. a boost [Fibonacci heap](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/12698431/boost-fibonacci-heap-decrease-operation).

But we're spending a lot of effort in keeping the indexes balanced, when in fact we will likely have a single element here for 99% of the cases (if my understanding is correct, please correct me if my assumptions are wrong) and we basically don't ever expect to have more than hundreds of elements, which would likely better fit in a cache line and probably be faster to store simply as a vector and lazily sort as late as possible instead of keeping them the indexes up-to-date and worrying about priority collisions.
A vector would make `PrivateBroadcast::Find` obsolete and allow us to edit the values and only update them as late as possible, while we could restructure the methods to be faster & simpler for 99% of the cases - while not being measurably slower for the worst case.

----


I went ahead and implemented the above, added a benchmark which exercises every public `PrivateBroadcast` method via txs from block413567.
The profiling confirms my suspicion that keeping track of two heavy indexes is both more complicated and slower than a simple `std::vector` solution. 

From the existing:
```C++
using ByTxid = std::unordered_map<Txid, TxWithPriority, SaltedTxidHasher>;
using ByPriority = std::multimap<Priority, Txid>;
using ByNodeId = std::unordered_map<NodeId, Txid>;
```
The first two are just optimizations for logarithmic operations - which look good on paper, but benchmarking reveals that they're dominated by heavy SipHash calculations.
If instead we stored all needed info in a struct such as:
```C++
struct Entry {
    CTransactionRef m_tx;
    Txid m_txid;
    Wtxid m_wtxid;
    Priority m_priority;

    Entry(const CTransactionRef& tx, Priority priority) : m_tx{tx},
                                                          m_txid{tx->GetHash()},
                                                          m_wtxid{tx->GetWitnessHash()},
                                                          m_priority{priority} {}

    friend bool operator<(const Entry& a, const Entry& b) { return a.m_priority < b.m_priority; }
};
```
and store each such tx in a continuous vector instead:
```C++
std::vector<Entry> m_entries GUARDED_BY(m_mutex);
```
we could indeed remove `PrivateBroadcast::Find` since we don't need to sync between the two additional indexes and the `Add`/`Remove`/`GetTxForBroadcast`/`GetTxPushedToNode`/`FinishBroadcast` would become linear to the number of total txs, simplifying the code considerably (~70% fewer lines, please feel free to reorganize) and considerably faster at the same time (~60%, according to the benchmark).

Benchmark code: https://github.com/l0rinc/bitcoin/commit/ec9c963ead87892b3fabd3f674d0b98160439242
Alternative implementation without indexes: https://github.com/l0rinc/bitcoin/commit/d42c922e9256625b166ab388ff234ba9bc272233#diff-ad1d5d83183a5facaf06e2b44c42a3e942a261b7506576720588bf6b158e4c3dL72

Note: the above commit also contains a different optimization pr (https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/33637/files#diff-23dc0fd9eb060143f0899dc7c7df087a79e1eb2a6600fcd4539b4ff31fe22fb9R202) but I'm not suggesting including that part in this PR.

I'm not sure the benchmark is fully representative, please correct it if you think it's not realistic.

As the [developer notes](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode-release-notes/xcode-16_3-release-notes) hint at, this probably isn't fixed in [*16.2*](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode-release-notes/xcode-16_2-release-notes) yet, only in [*16.3*](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode-release-notes/xcode-16_3-release-notes).

And it seems https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/33932 doesn't change the situation since we're [deliberately downloading](https://github.com/l0rinc/bitcoin/actions/runs/19633894569/job/56224901024?pr=57#step:9:1265) version 15 here:
> 22.23 + retry -- curl --location --fail https://bitcoincore.org/depends-sources/sdks/Xcode-15.0-15A240d-extracted-SDK-with-libcxx-headers.tar.gz -o /home/runner/work/_temp/depends/sdk-sources/Xcode-15.0-15A240d-extracted-SDK-with-libcxx-headers.tar.gz

----

Getting back to the original suggestion, if we can't rely on full C++20 support, maybe we can simplify the comparison to:
```C++
struct Priority {
    size_t num_broadcasted{0};
    NodeClock::time_point last_broadcasted{};

    // Fewer broadcasts first, then older last_broadcasted first.
    bool operator<(const Priority& other) const
    {
        return std::tie(num_broadcasted, last_broadcasted) <
               std::tie(other.num_broadcasted, other.last_broadcasted);
    }
};
```

* failure with defaulted comparison: https://github.com/l0rinc/bitcoin/actions/runs/19631424247/job/56213071892?pr=56
* passes with `std::tie`: https://github.com/l0rinc/bitcoin/actions/runs/19637986389/job/56236648004?pr=56

